  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n3 3 3 3 3 3\n3 2 2 2 2 3\n3 2 0 0 2 3\n3 2 0 0 2 3\n3 2 2 2 2 3\n3 3 3 3 3 3\nOutput:\n0 0 0 0 0 0\n0 3 3 3 3 0\n0 3 2 2 3 0\n0 3 2 2 3 0\n0 3 3 3 3 0\n0 0 0 0 0 0\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n0 0 0 0 0 0\n0 7 7 7 7 0\n0 7 6 6 7 0\n0 7 6 6 7 0\n0 7 7 7 7 0\n0 0 0 0 0 0\nOutput:\n6 6 6 6 6 6\n6 0 0 0 0 6\n6 0 7 7 0 6\n6 0 7 7 0 6\n6 0 0 0 0 6\n6 6 6 6 6 6\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n8 8 8 8 8 8 8 8\n8 0 0 0 0 0 0 8\n8 0 5 5 5 5 0 8\n8 0 5 8 8 5 0 8\n8 0 5 8 8 5 0 8\n8 0 5 5 5 5 0 8\n8 0 0 0 0 0 0 8\n8 8 8 8 8 8 8 8\nOutput:\n5 5 5 5 5 5 5 5\n5 8 8 8 8 8 8 5\n5 8 0 0 0 0 8 5\n5 8 0 5 5 0 8 5\n5 8 0 5 5 0 8 5\n5 8 0 0 0 0 8 5\n5 8 8 8 8 8 8 5\n5 5 5 5 5 5 5 5\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n9 9 9 9 9 9\n9 0 0 0 0 9\n9 0 1 1 0 9\n9 0 1 1 0 9\n9 0 0 0 0 9\n9 9 9 9 9 9\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform a grid that consists of concentric rectangular frames.\n    The colors of the distinct frames are rotated outward by one position:\n    the innermost distinct color becomes the outermost frame,\n    each other distinct color moves one frame outward,\n    and any extra inner frames (caused by repeated colors) are filled\n    with the new outermost color.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n\n    h = len(grid)\n    w = len(grid[0]) if h else 0\n\n    # ---- collect colors of each concentric layer ----\n    layer_colors = []\n    top, bottom = 0, h - 1\n    left, right = 0, w - 1\n    while top <= bottom and left <= right:\n        # the current outermost cell determines the color of this layer\n        layer_colors.append(grid[top][left])\n        top += 1\n        bottom -= 1\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n\n    # ---- build the list of distinct colors in order of appearance ----\n    distinct = []\n    for col in layer_colors:\n        if col not in distinct:\n            distinct.append(col)\n\n    # ---- rotate the distinct colors outward (innermost \u2192 outermost) ----\n    if distinct:\n        rotated = [distinct[-1]] + distinct[:-1]\n    else:\n        rotated = []\n\n    # ---- map each layer index to its new color ----\n    # number of layers = len(layer_colors)\n    num_layers = len(layer_colors)\n    mapping = []\n    for i in range(num_layers):\n        if i < len(rotated):\n            mapping.append(rotated[i])\n        else:\n            # extra inner layers (caused by repeated colors) get the new outermost color\n            mapping.append(rotated[0])\n\n    # ---- construct the output grid using the computed mapping ----\n    out = [[0] * w for _ in range(h)]\n    for r in range(h):\n        for c in range(w):\n            # layer index = minimal distance to any edge\n            idx = min(r, c, h - 1 - r, w - 1 - c)\n            out[r][c] = mapping[idx]\n\n    return out",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform a grid that consists of concentric rectangular frames.\n    The colors of the distinct frames are rotated outward by one position:\n    the innermost distinct color becomes the outermost frame,\n    each other distinct color moves one frame outward,\n    and any extra inner frames (caused by repeated colors) are filled\n    with the new outermost color.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n\n    h = len(grid)\n    w = len(grid[0]) if h else 0\n\n    # ---- collect colors of each concentric layer ----\n    layer_colors = []\n    top, bottom = 0, h - 1\n    left, right = 0, w - 1\n    while top <= bottom and left <= right:\n        # the current outermost cell determines the color of this layer\n        layer_colors.append(grid[top][left])\n        top += 1\n        bottom -= 1\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n\n    # ---- build the list of distinct colors in order of appearance ----\n    distinct = []\n    for col in layer_colors:\n        if col not in distinct:\n            distinct.append(col)\n\n    # ---- rotate the distinct colors outward (innermost \u2192 outermost) ----\n    if distinct:\n        rotated = [distinct[-1]] + distinct[:-1]\n    else:\n        rotated = []\n\n    # ---- map each layer index to its new color ----\n    # number of layers = len(layer_colors)\n    num_layers = len(layer_colors)\n    mapping = []\n    for i in range(num_layers):\n        if i < len(rotated):\n            mapping.append(rotated[i])\n        else:\n            # extra inner layers (caused by repeated colors) get the new outermost color\n            mapping.append(rotated[0])\n\n    # ---- construct the output grid using the computed mapping ----\n    out = [[0] * w for _ in range(h)]\n    for r in range(h):\n        for c in range(w):\n            # layer index = minimal distance to any edge\n            idx = min(r, c, h - 1 - r, w - 1 - c)\n            out[r][c] = mapping[idx]\n\n    return out",
